the quest to understand the laws of  nature the deepest possible level has  been maybe one of the noblest and most  consequential aspirations of human  beings for for centuries now and in  fundamental physics we've understood  that to do this we have to go to the  extremes so we study the laws of nature  what the universe looks like on the  longest possible distances the largest  possible distances in cosmology but also  at the very very smallest possible  distances where we discover that matter  is made up out of elementary particles  that interact with each other  governed by fundamental forces and  rather surprisingly and very importantly  in the last hundred years we've learned  that what seems to be a dizzying array  of very different phenomenon turned out  to be much more closely related to each  other than would appear at first sight  and that these essential simplicity and  unity and and really beauty of the laws  of nature only reveal themselves when  you study nature under a microscope and  you and you look at what's happening at  very short distances or very high  energies so we've been going to this  short distance frontier high-energy  frontier for for four century now and  there's absolutely no reason to stop in  fact a machine very similar to the  machine that's being talked about now  was being entertained in the United  States already in the early 1990s a  superconducting supercollider the u.s.  tragically decided not to proceed with  that project but it's something we know  how to do the technology exists the  know-how exists and the scale of  resources that we're talking about is  not beyond the pale of of similar  projects in the past so that's the big  picture answer to this question we need  to build this collider because because  we can and because it represents the  frontier of what we've been doing and  studying the fundamental laws of physics  for centuries now
 

